  "final_text": "I submitted **holistic** with confidence 0.8. I used 49 of the 60 budget units. The test results were very noisy (the same task ranged from 1 to 65 examples), so this rests on averages over repeated tests.\n\nI tracked one probe task, X = `L2-S1-M2`, through three stages. \"Overlap\" below means the fraction of inputs on which two tasks give the same response.\n\n| Stage | What was trained | X's rules | X's highest overlap with a trained task | X mean cost (number of tests) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline | nothing | all new | \u2013 | 46 (5) |\n| Phase 1 | `L1-S1-M2`, `L2-S2-M1` | each seen once | 0 | 41 (4) |\n| Phase 2 | 6 more tasks | each seen exactly 3 times | 0.375 | 30 (16) |\n\nFor comparison:\n- **A task it was trained on** (`L1-S1-M2`) cost about 9 examples (6 tests).\n- **A control task, Z = `L4-S4-M2`**, had the same highest overlap (0.375) as X in phase 2. But two of its rules had appeared only once in training, so they would not have become reusable parts. Z cost 31 on average (10 tests), the same as X.\n\n**How this rules things out:**\n- **Part-based is unlikely.** After phase 1 all of X's rules were known, so a part-based learner should have found X about as easy as a task it was trained on (about 9). X stayed near the untrained level (41).\n- **Factor-when-justified is unlikely.** After phase 2 each of X's rules had appeared in 3 training tasks, enough for k = 2 or k = 3. That learner should have found X much easier than Z. They cost the same.\n- **Holistic fits.** X got cheaper as its overlap with stored tasks rose from 0 to 0.375. X and Z, which had the same overlap, cost the same.\n\nThe 0.8 confidence reflects the noise. The X-versus-Z comparison has an uncertainty of roughly \u00b18 examples, so a smaller factor-when-justified effect than I expected could have been hidden.",
